<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yarkand</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yarkant, Shache</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yarkand, also called Shache, is an oasis city in the south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, on the Yarkand River. A station on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> routes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, it was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarkent-khanate">Yarkent Khanate</a> from 1514 until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungar</a> conquest of 1680, and later a major town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgaria">Kashgaria</a> under Qing rule. Its people are mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yarkand.jpg" alt="Yarkand" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Colegota · CC BY-SA 2.5 es · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yarkand-calles-d01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1680 the cities of Altishahr, among them Kashgar, <b>Yarkand</b> and Khotan, paid the Dzungars substantial tribute in grain, cloth and silver.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Hunza's Burushaski-speaking people farmed irrigated terraces and, until the late 19th century, raided caravans on the routes between Ladakh and <b>Yarkand</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/hunza-state">Hunza State</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1514 Sultan Said Khan, a descendant of Yunus Khan of Moghulistan, took Kashgar and <b>Yarkand</b> from the Dughlat amir Mirza Aba Bakr, founding a khanate with Yarkand as its capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/yarkent-khanate">Yarkent Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Yaqub Beg soon sidelined Buzurg Khan, conquered Kashgar, <b>Yarkand</b> and Khotan, and by 1870 controlled most of the Tarim Basin, including Turpan and Urumqi.</blockquote><a href="/state/yettishar">Yettishar</a></li></ul></section>
